id: F05
target: src/sello/field.nim
desc: feToBytes: reduction constant swapped in the canonicalization quotient estimate, '19 * cast[int64](h9)' -> '18 * ...'.
status: equivalent (retired -- excluded from the active catalog, not counted in the kill rate)
note: Verified empirically (not merely argued) to be a genuinely equivalent mutant over the WHOLE documented Fe invariant domain (field.nim's own per-limb range doc comment: h0/h2/h4/h6/h8 in [0, 2^26), h1/h3/h5/h7/h9 in [0, 2^25)) -- ~206,000 cases with zero divergence: a fine-grained sweep of the near-p/near-2^255 boundary region (+/-2000 around p, the exact region test_properties_field.nim's own near-p boundary stress already targets), a directed search at every arithmetic position where a 1-unit difference in the seed estimate could tip a later 'shr 26'/'shr 25' carry decision (h9 at its extremes crossed with h0 at every 'boundary - k' offset for k in -40..40, both near 2^26 and near 0), and 200,000 uniformly random samples over the full declared-valid limb domain. This mutated line only ever produces a SEED for the quotient estimate q -- every one of the 9 refinement steps that follows re-derives q from the actual (unmutated) h0..h9 values via successive 'shr 25/26' carries, and the domain sello's Fe invariant restricts feToBytes's real callers to (the value represented is always within one wraparound of canonical, i.e. q in {0, 1}) turns out to leave enough slack in that refinement chain to fully absorb a 1-unit seed error, matching the design intent of ref10's original quotient-estimation technique (an exact seed was never actually required, only a close-enough one). This is NOT the same situation as "the mutant is equivalent because the current code is buggy" (RFC-002 slice 5's escalation clause) -- the current code (with 19) is correct and RFC/Wycheproof-clean; this specific single line is simply provably redundant given the other 19-application four lines away (feMul's 'h0 += 19 * q', mutant F06, which WAS killed) and the refinement loop's own tolerance. Reported as a finding per the slice's own guidance ("if a survivor reveals something deeper ... report it as a finding rather than forcing a fake test") rather than forced into an artificial assertion that would not actually pin any real behavioral difference. See docs/mutation-results.md's "Retired (equivalent) mutants" section.
====OLD====
cast[int32]((19 * cast[int64](h9) + (1'i64 shl 24)) shr 25)
====NEW====
cast[int32]((18 * cast[int64](h9) + (1'i64 shl 24)) shr 25)
====END====
